\newlabel{fig:alignment_vs_skew_symmetry}{{1}{4}{\textbf {Alignmnent vs. Skew Symmetry} Environments charted by their alignment based on the ratio of the norm of $K$ to the norm of $S$ and the relative performance of exact TD vs. exact E-Minimization $\frac {\text {AUC}(MSE_E)}{\text {AUC}(MSE_{TD})}$. The negative slope captures the fact that in more skew-symmetric environments, $E$ has better value learning. 10 seeds}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:alignment_vs_skew_symmetry@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][3][]4}}
\newlabel{tab:table4_env_win_rate_triad_k64}{{1}{4}{Win Rate of TD, MC, and E minimization across several metrics: The AUC and final MSE as well as two measures of policy improvement: (1) the cosine similarity between the learned and true advantage functions, and (2) the value of the greedy policy at the start staet. For each environment, an algorithm is a winner if it performs best on a certain metric. Ties occur when no single algorithm was statistically significantly best, and are split evenly. Thus $1.5/9$ indicates that the algorithm won outright on exactly one environment and tied on another}{table.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{tab:table4_env_win_rate_triad_k64@cref}{{[table][1][]1}{[1][3][]4}}
